IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `oapa-nouns`

-- Offences against the Person Act 1861 -- LAYER 1: HOMICIDE (the heading "Homicide",
-- sections 1 to 11).
--
-- What this part of the text actually contains: section 4 (soliciting a murder),
-- section 5 (the penalty for manslaughter, with no definition of it), and sections 9
-- and 10 (where a murder or manslaughter may be tried).  Sections 1, 2, 6, 7 and 11
-- are rows of dots in the deposited text.  The text therefore never says what MURDER
-- or MANSLAUGHTER is; sections 4, 5, 9 and 10 use the two words without defining them.
-- The encoding takes each as a fact the person states ("what happened amounts to a
-- murder") and answers `the deposited text does not say` where the question is
-- whether it IS one.

§ `Homicide`

§§ `Sections 1 and 2: blank in this text`

-- src:6 | 1 . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .
-- src:7 | 2 . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .
@ref Offences against the Person Act 1861 ss 1, 2 (src:6-7)
GIVETH A STRING
`ss 1 and 2: blank in this text` MEANS
    "Sections 1 and 2 show only their numbers and a row of dots; there is nothing to encode."

-- The text never defines murder: sections 1 and 2 are blank.  This is the ENCODER's
-- answer ("the deposited text does not say"), returned so that no rule can quietly
-- answer "is what happened a murder?" with FALSE.  Sections 4, 9 and 10 use the word
-- and take it as a fact the person states.
@ref Offences against the Person Act 1861 ss 1, 2 (src:6-7)
GIVETH A `What the Act says`
`whether what happened is a murder, as this text defines murder` MEANS
    `the deposited text does not say`

§§ `Section 4: conspiring or soliciting to commit murder`

-- src:8 | 4 Conspiring or soliciting to commit murder.
-- src:9 | . . . Whosoever shall solicit, encourage, persuade, or endeavour to persuade, or shall propose to any person, to murder any other person, whether he be a subject of Her Majesty or not, and whether he be within the Queen’s dominions or not, shall be guilty of a misdemeanor, and being convicted thereof shall be liable . . . to imprisonment for life . . .
@ref Offences against the Person Act 1861 s 4 (src:8-9)
-- The five things the section lists the person as doing "to any person", each a way
-- of putting the idea of a murder to somebody.  Plain gloss of each verb:
--   solicit      = ask for it; encourage = urge or hearten them towards it;
--   persuade     = win them over to it;  endeavour to persuade = try to win them over,
--   whether or not it works;  propose = put it to them as something to be done.
-- "whether he be a subject of Her Majesty or not, and whether he be within the
-- Queen's dominions or not": the two facts about the intended victim are carried in
-- the record only so that the encoding can show that no rule reads them.
DECLARE `Conduct under section 4` HAS
    `solicited a person to murder another person` IS A BOOLEAN
    `encouraged a person to murder another person` IS A BOOLEAN
    `persuaded a person to murder another person` IS A BOOLEAN
    `endeavoured to persuade a person to murder another person` IS A BOOLEAN
    `proposed to a person that they murder another person` IS A BOOLEAN
    `what was put to that person would be a murder` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the other person is a subject of Her Majesty` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the other person is within the Queen's dominions` IS A BOOLEAN

@ref Offences against the Person Act 1861 s 4 (src:8-9)
GIVEN c IS A `Conduct under section 4`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 4: soliciting or proposing a murder` c MEANS
        (   c's `solicited a person to murder another person`
         OR c's `encouraged a person to murder another person`
         OR c's `persuaded a person to murder another person`
         OR c's `endeavoured to persuade a person to murder another person`
         OR c's `proposed to a person that they murder another person`)
    AND c's `what was put to that person would be a murder`

@ref Offences against the Person Act 1861 s 4 (src:9)
GIVETH A Classification
`s 4: what the text calls the offence` MEANS misdemeanor

-- "shall be liable . . . to imprisonment for life . . .": the ceiling is life.
@ref Offences against the Person Act 1861 s 4 (src:9)
GIVETH A LIST OF Sentence
`s 4: the sentences the text states` MEANS
    LIST `imprisonment` `for life`

§§ `Section 5: manslaughter`

-- src:10 | 5 Manslaughter.
-- src:11 | Whosoever shall be convicted of manslaughter shall be liable, at the discretion of the court, to be kept in penal servitude for life . . .
@ref Offences against the Person Act 1861 s 5 (src:10-11)
GIVETH A Classification
`s 5: what the text calls the offence` MEANS `no classification stated`

-- "Whosoever shall be convicted of manslaughter shall be liable, at the discretion of
-- the court, to be kept in penal servitude for life . . .".  The section states a
-- PENALTY FOR A CONVICTION and no element of the offence; the choice within the
-- ceiling is the court's.
@ref Offences against the Person Act 1861 s 5 (src:11)
GIVEN `the person has been convicted of manslaughter` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A LIST OF Sentence
`s 5: the sentences the text states for a conviction of manslaughter` `the person has been convicted of manslaughter` MEANS
    IF `the person has been convicted of manslaughter`
    THEN LIST `penal servitude` `for life`
    ELSE EMPTY

-- The same for manslaughter: section 5 states a penalty for a conviction and no
-- element, so whether what happened IS a manslaughter is not answered by this text.
@ref Offences against the Person Act 1861 s 5 (src:10-11)
GIVETH A `What the Act says`
`whether what happened is a manslaughter, as this text defines manslaughter` MEANS
    `the deposited text does not say`

§§ `Sections 6 and 7: blank in this text`

-- src:12 | 6 . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .
-- src:13 | 7 . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .
@ref Offences against the Person Act 1861 ss 6, 7 (src:12-13)
GIVETH A STRING
`ss 6 and 7: blank in this text` MEANS
    "Sections 6 and 7 show only their numbers and a row of dots; there is nothing to encode."

§§ `Section 9: murder or manslaughter abroad`

-- src:14 | 9 Murder or manslaughter abroad.
-- src:15 | Where any murder or manslaughter shall be committed on land out of the United Kingdom, whether within the Queen’s dominions or without, and whether the person killed were a subject of Her Majesty or not, every offence committed by any subject of Her Majesty in respect of any such case, whether the same shall amount to the offence of murder or of manslaughter, . . . , may be dealt with, inquired of, tried, determined, and punished . . . in England or Ireland . . . : Provided, that nothing herein contained shall prevent any person from being tried in any place out of England or Ireland for any murder or manslaughter committed out of England or Ireland, in the same manner as such person might have been tried before the passing of this Act.
@ref Offences against the Person Act 1861 s 9 (src:14-15)
-- "subject of Her Majesty" is a term the Act does not define, asked as a fact.
-- "on land out of the United Kingdom": the killing itself was on land, outside the
-- United Kingdom.  "whether within the Queen's dominions or without, and whether the
-- person killed were a subject of Her Majesty or not" are the two facts the text says
-- do not matter; they are carried so that a test can show no rule reads them.
DECLARE `Killing abroad under section 9` HAS
    `a murder or manslaughter was committed` IS A BOOLEAN
    `on land out of the United Kingdom` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the person charged is a subject of Her Majesty` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the place is within the Queen's dominions` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the person killed was a subject of Her Majesty` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the killing was out of England or Ireland` IS A BOOLEAN

-- "every offence committed by any subject of Her Majesty in respect of any such case,
-- whether the same shall amount to the offence of murder or of manslaughter, . . . ,
-- may be dealt with, inquired of, tried, determined, and punished . . . in England or
-- Ireland".  This is a power of the courts of England or Ireland and a liability of
-- the person charged.
@ref Offences against the Person Act 1861 s 9 (src:15)
GIVEN k IS A `Killing abroad under section 9`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 9: the person may be dealt with, tried and punished in England or Ireland` k MEANS
        k's `a murder or manslaughter was committed`
    AND k's `on land out of the United Kingdom`
    AND k's `the person charged is a subject of Her Majesty`

-- The proviso: "nothing herein contained shall prevent any person from being tried in
-- any place out of England or Ireland for any murder or manslaughter committed out of
-- England or Ireland, in the same manner as such person might have been tried before
-- the passing of this Act".  The section leaves a trial elsewhere open; it does not
-- say how such a trial was done before the Act, and the encoding does not guess.
@ref Offences against the Person Act 1861 s 9 (src:15)
GIVEN k IS A `Killing abroad under section 9`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 9: a trial out of England or Ireland is not prevented by this Act` k MEANS
        k's `a murder or manslaughter was committed`
    AND k's `the killing was out of England or Ireland`

§§ `Section 10: where the death or its cause happens in England or Ireland`

-- src:16 | 10 Provision for the trial of murder and manslaughter where the death or cause of death only happens in England or Ireland.
-- src:17 | Where any person being criminally stricken, poisoned, or otherwise hurt upon the sea, or at any place out of England or Ireland, shall die of such stroke, poisoning, or hurt in England or Ireland, or, being criminally stricken, poisoned, or otherwise hurt in any place in England or Ireland, shall die of such stroke, poisoning, or hurt upon the sea, or at any place out of England or Ireland, every offence committed in respect of any such case, whether the same shall amount to the offence of murder or of manslaughter, . . . , may be dealt with, inquired of, tried, determined, and punished . . . in England or Ireland . . .
@ref Offences against the Person Act 1861 s 10 (src:16-17)
-- The three places the section distinguishes: "in England or Ireland", "upon the
-- sea", and "at any place out of England or Ireland" other than the sea.
DECLARE Place IS ONE OF
    `in England or Ireland`
    `upon the sea`
    `at a place out of England or Ireland, other than the sea`

@ref Offences against the Person Act 1861 s 10 (src:16-17)
DECLARE `Hurt and death under section 10` HAS
    `a person was criminally stricken, poisoned or otherwise hurt` IS A BOOLEAN
    `where the person was hurt` IS A Place
    `the person died of that stroke, poisoning or hurt` IS A BOOLEAN
    `where the person died` IS A Place
    `the offence amounts to murder or manslaughter` IS A BOOLEAN

-- A place is "out" when it is the sea or any other place out of England or Ireland.
@ref Offences against the Person Act 1861 s 10 (src:17)
GIVEN p IS A Place
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 10: the place is out of England and Ireland` p MEANS
    CONSIDER p
    WHEN `in England or Ireland` THEN FALSE
    WHEN `upon the sea` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `at a place out of England or Ireland, other than the sea` THEN TRUE

-- Hurt out of England and Ireland with death in England or Ireland, OR hurt in
-- England or Ireland with death out of them: "every offence committed in respect of
-- any such case, whether the same shall amount to the offence of murder or of
-- manslaughter, . . . , may be dealt with, inquired of, tried, determined, and
-- punished . . . in England or Ireland".
@ref Offences against the Person Act 1861 s 10 (src:17)
GIVEN h IS A `Hurt and death under section 10`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 10: the person may be dealt with, tried and punished in England or Ireland` h MEANS
        h's `a person was criminally stricken, poisoned or otherwise hurt`
    AND h's `the person died of that stroke, poisoning or hurt`
    AND h's `the offence amounts to murder or manslaughter`
    AND (   (    `s 10: the place is out of England and Ireland` (h's `where the person was hurt`)
             AND NOT (`s 10: the place is out of England and Ireland` (h's `where the person died`)))
         OR (    NOT (`s 10: the place is out of England and Ireland` (h's `where the person was hurt`))
             AND `s 10: the place is out of England and Ireland` (h's `where the person died`)))

§§ `Section 11: blank in this text`

-- src:18 | 11 . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .
@ref Offences against the Person Act 1861 s 11 (src:18)
GIVETH A STRING
`s 11: blank in this text` MEANS
    "Section 11 shows only its number and a row of dots; there is nothing to encode."
